#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133396 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "90adf237-9627-55f1-8f90-8fbc4acf02fc" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Triple Integral Over a Type II Region"}] :question/id #uuid "2d4b2c66-5600-5712-8ec9-3d7325b247c2" :question/math-academy-id "e-11348" :question/problem "Evaluate the triple integral\n\n$\\displaystyle \\iiint\\limits_R 4x^3 \\: \\mathrm{d}V$\n\nwhere the region $R$ is given by\n$R = \\left\\{(x,y,z) \\:: \\: (y,z) \\in D, \\: 0 \\leq x \\leq \\sqrt{y} \\right\\}$,\nand $D$ is a finite region in $yz$-plane enclosed between the $y$-axis, the $z$-axis, and the line $z = 4-y$ in the first quadrant." :question/worked-solution "Writing down our triple integral as a mixed integral, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\iiint \\limits_{R} 4x^3 \\, \\textrm{d}V = \\iint\\limits_{D} \\left[ \\int_{0}^{\\sqrt{y}} 4x^3 \\, \\mathrm{d}x \\right] \\mathrm{d}A\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral with respect to $x$, treating $y$ and $z$ as constants:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∬_(D)[∫_{0}^{\\sqrt{y}}4x^{3}dx]dA &= ∬_(D)[x^{4}]_{x = 0}^{x = \\sqrt{y}}dA \\\\\n&= ∬_(D)(\\sqrt{y})^{4}dA \\\\\n&= ∬_(D)y^{2}dA\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe must now evaluate this double integral over the region $D$ in the $yz$-plane shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2141/Source/Images/f91b4d0b9ed33b8d327459a73fe31766.png)\n\nWe can evaluate our double integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∬_(D)y^{2}dA &= ∫_{0}^{4}∫_{0}^{4 - y}y^{2}dzdy \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{4}[∫_{0}^{4 - y}y^{2}dz]dy \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{4}[y^{2}z]_{z = 0}^{z = 4 - y}dy \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{4}y^{2}(4 - y)dy \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{4}4y^{2} - y^{3}dy \\\\\n&= [\\frac{4}{3}y^{3} - \\frac{1}{4}y^{4}]_{y = 0}^{y = 4} \\\\\n&= \\frac{4}{3}(64 - 0) - \\frac{1}{4}(256 - 0) \\\\\n&= \\frac{64}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133397 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0bd48398-cb9d-579f-b7f7-0f6be19172ce" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Mixed Integral"}] :question/id #uuid "295afdb2-2e90-5f1a-8830-d3886d99fbad" :question/math-academy-id "e-11349" :question/problem "Evaluate the mixed integral\n$\\displaystyle \\iint\\limits_D \\left[\\int_{0}^{z} \\dfrac{\\cos (2y)}{1-y} \\,\\textrm{d}x \\right] \\textrm{d}A$\nwhere the region $D$ in the $yz$-plane is given by\n$D = \\left\\{(y,z) \\,: \\, 0 \\leq y \\leq \\dfrac{\\pi}{6}, \\: 0 \\leq z \\leq 2\\sqrt{1-y} \\right\\}$." :question/worked-solution "First, we evaluate the inner integral with respect to $x$, treating $y$ and $z$ as constants:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∬_(D)[∫_{0}^{z}(\\cos (2y))/(1 - y)dx]dA &= ∬_(D)(\\cos (2y))/(1 - y)[x]_{x = 0}^{x = z}dA \\\\\n&= ∬_(D)(\\cos (2y))/(1 - y)(z - 0)dA \\\\\n&= ∬_(D)(z\\cos (2y))/(1 - y)dA\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the double integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∬_(D)(z\\cos (2y))/(1 - y)dA &= ∫_{0}^{π/6}∫_{0}^{2\\sqrt{1 - y}}(z\\cos (2y))/(1 - y)dzdy \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{π/6}(\\cos (2y))/(1 - y)[\\frac{z^{2}}{2}]_{z = 0}^{z = 2\\sqrt{1 - y}}dy \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{π/6}(\\cos (2y))/(1 - y)((4(1 - y))/(2) - 0)dy \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{π/6}2\\cos (2y)dy \\\\\n&= [\\sin (2y)]_{y = 0}^{y = π/6} \\\\\n&= \\sin (\\frac{π}{3}) - \\sin (0) \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133403 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e612d4ba-b772-5f56-a031-fdfc05d63221" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Triple Integral Over a Type II Region Given a Diagram"}] :question/id #uuid "ad3e88cb-f213-509b-93c5-3fc529d617c0" :question/math-academy-id "e-12369" :question/problem "Evaluate the triple integral\n$\\displaystyle \\iiint\\limits_R 12z^2 \\: \\mathrm{d}V$\nover the region $R$, shown above.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2141/Source/Images/e1e0a37a01b5ecf240851daf1ff21f56.png)\n\n*Hint: All surfaces, except for the front one, are parallel to the corresponding coordinate planes.*" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2141/Source/Images/a4f8d3e95c46f652b90983ba8bfa9d97.png)\n\nNotice that our solid can be written as the type II region\n\n$$\nR = \\big\\{(x,y,z) \\:: \\: (y,z) \\in D, \\:\\: 0 \\leq x \\leq 3-y-z \\big\\}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nD = \\big\\{(y,z) \\:: \\: 0 \\leq y \\leq 1, \\:\\: 0 \\leq z \\leq 1 \\big\\}\n$$\n\nis the projection of $R$ onto the $yz$-plane, shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2141/Source/Images/5b209d6360c4381ea8e24a7a857c8cba.png)\n\nHence, we can express the region $R$ as\n\n$$\nR = \\big\\{(x,y,z) \\:: \\: 0 \\leq y \\leq 1, \\:\\: 0 \\leq z \\leq 1, \\:\\: 0 \\leq x \\leq 3-y-z \\big\\}\n$$\n\nAs a result, by writing down our triple integral as a repeated integral, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\iiint \\limits_{R} 12z^2 \\: \\textrm{d}V = \\int_{0}^{1} \\int_{0}^{1} \\int_{0}^{3-y-z} 12z^2 \\: \\mathrm{d}x \\: \\mathrm{d}z \\: \\mathrm{d}y\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral with respect to $x$, treating $y$ and $z$ as constants:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_{0}^{1}∫_{0}^{1}∫_{0}^{3 - y - z}12z^{2}dxdzdy &= ∫_{0}^{1}∫_{0}^{1}[∫_{0}^{3 - y - z}12z^{2}dx]dzdy \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{1}∫_{0}^{1}12z^{2}[x]_{x = 0}^{x = 3 - y - z}dzdy \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{1}∫_{0}^{1}12z^{2}(3 - y - z)dzdy \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{1}∫_{0}^{1}(36z^{2} - 12yz^{2} - 12z^{3})dzdy\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the inner integral with respect to $z$, treating $y$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_{0}^{1}∫_{0}^{1}(36z^{2} - 12yz^{2} - 12z^{3})dzdy &= ∫_{0}^{1}[∫_{0}^{1}(36z^{2} - 12yz^{2} - 12z^{3})dz]dy \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{1}[12z^{3} - 4yz^{3} - 3z^{4}]_{z = 0}^{z = 1}dy \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{1}(12 - 4y - 3)dy \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{1}(9 - 4y)dy\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate with respect to $y{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_{0}^{1}(9 - 4y)dy &= [9y - 2y^{2}]_{y = 0}^{y = 1} \\\\\n&= (9 - 2) - 0 \\\\\n&= 7\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133405 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a22ed84a-4b6f-53b3-8336-bf592aed8686" :knowledge-point/title "Representing a Triple Integral as a Mixed or Repeated Integral"}] :question/id #uuid "b51667a8-f48a-59a2-b09e-59c6fcad5507" :question/math-academy-id "e-12398" :question/problem "Find the missing limits in the repeated integral below\n\n$\\displaystyle \\iiint \\limits_{R} f(x,y,z) \\: \\textrm{d}V = \\int_{\\ast}^{\\ast} \\int_{\\ast}^{\\ast} \\int_{\\ast}^{\\ast} f(x,y,z) \\: \\mathrm{d}x \\: \\mathrm{d}z \\: \\mathrm{d}y$\n\nwhere $R$ is the type II region defined as\n\n$R=\\left\\{(x,y,z) \\:: \\: 1 \\leq y \\leq 4, \\: -y^2 \\leq z \\leq \\sqrt{y}, \\: -2 \\leq x \\leq ye^{z+2} \\right\\}$." :question/worked-solution "A type II region in three-dimensional space can be written as\n\n$$\nR = \\big\\{(x,y,z) \\:: \\: (y,z) \\in D, \\: u_1(y,z) \\leq x \\leq u_2(y,z) \\big\\}\n$$\n\nA typical three-dimensional type II region can be visualized schematically, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2141/Source/Images/83d78c3a80d9921c38438e2967052d69.png)\n\nIf $R$ is a type II region, we can express the triple integral of $f(x,y,z)$ over $R$ as a mixed integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\iiint\\limits_{R} f(x,y,z)\\,\\textrm d V = \\iint\\limits_{D} \\left[\\int_{u_1(y,z)}^{u_2(y,z)} f(x,y,z)\\,\\textrm{d}x \\right]\\,\\textrm d A\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nu_1(y,z) = -2, \\qquad u_2(y,z) = ye^{z+2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our triple integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\iiint \\limits_{R} f(x,y,z) \\: \\textrm{d}V = \\iint\\limits_{D} \\left[ \\int_{-2}^{ye^{z+2}} f(x,y,z) \\: \\mathrm{d}x \\right] \\mathrm{d}A\n$$\n\nNow, the region $D$ is given by\n\n$$\nD =\\left\\{(x,y) \\:: \\: 1 \\leq y \\leq 4, \\: -y^2 \\leq z \\leq \\sqrt{y} \\right\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by expressing the double integral over $D$ as a repeated integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∬_(D)[∫_{-2}^{ye^{z + 2}}f(x, y, z)dx]dA &= ∫_{1}^{4}∫_{-y^{2}}^{\\sqrt{y}}[∫_{-2}^{ye^{z + 2}}f(x, y, z)dx]dzdy \\\\\n&= ∫_{1}^{4}∫_{-y^{2}}^{\\sqrt{y}}∫_{-2}^{ye^{z + 2}}f(x, y, z)dxdzdy\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134193 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "90adf237-9627-55f1-8f90-8fbc4acf02fc" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Triple Integral Over a Type II Region"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d488ebe0-9dde-539d-9806-021bc1b09cd5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131601} {:answer/id #uuid "02d23ad9-b8b7-58fd-8c6f-449d308835c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131602} {:answer/id #uuid "0c90dde8-ae27-59ec-9c01-2274aa4d6671" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131603} {:answer/id #uuid "36e1062b-0e17-5f5f-96c5-030f88ec8cac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131604} {:answer/id #uuid "6eb1dd30-f4f1-55b7-acff-5b7c33f91bb1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131605}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d488ebe0-9dde-539d-9806-021bc1b09cd5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131601} :answer-field/id #uuid "298c69f2-8ca2-54cb-980e-894b604ba719" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131600}] :question/id #uuid "02ff2794-de30-53c1-a133-ec1abbd3f0b9" :question/math-academy-id "q-40208" :question/problem "Evaluate the triple integral\n\n$∭_(R)2ydV$\n\nwhere the region $R$ is given by\n$R = {(x, y, z): (y, z) ∈ D, 0 \\le x \\le z\\sqrt{3}}$,\nand $D$ is a finite region in $yz$-plane enclosed between the $y$-axes, the line $y = 2$ and the line $z = y$ in the first quadrant."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134196 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e612d4ba-b772-5f56-a031-fdfc05d63221" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Triple Integral Over a Type II Region Given a Diagram"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8d07b9ef-58af-527d-847e-ae7482a3e790" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{40}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131619} {:answer/id #uuid "a30e89de-0337-5ffc-9dad-b0b68f78df67" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{52}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131620} {:answer/id #uuid "dfe90321-97df-58a9-8ef1-32b725cc649b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131621} {:answer/id #uuid "05034627-feaa-51bc-8ffa-542dfc4790d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131622} {:answer/id #uuid "3b1739f8-d406-5653-adf3-596aee499f7f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131623}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a30e89de-0337-5ffc-9dad-b0b68f78df67" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{52}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131620} :answer-field/id #uuid "e99a475d-172e-5f35-8c7f-246a2fffd73f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131618}] :question/id #uuid "e1a1cf7f-cf9c-51a1-babc-d27035fc1ca5" :question/math-academy-id "q-40219" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2141/Source/Images/q-40219.png)\n\nEvaluate the triple integral\n$∭_(R)4ydV$\nover the region $R$, shown above.\n\n*Hint: All surfaces, except for the front one, are parallel to the corresponding coordinate planes.*"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134210 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0bd48398-cb9d-579f-b7f7-0f6be19172ce" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Mixed Integral"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c2c163b1-1d8c-5353-8878-3b13afaff8a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131703} {:answer/id #uuid "c331fd74-3fe7-5f07-aff0-9d0fccfed4f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131704} {:answer/id #uuid "e96718c2-1174-5d23-a5bd-e18ba9605bd9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "72" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131705} {:answer/id #uuid "f80d2460-7c35-5d8f-be8c-aee3a6d918a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131706} {:answer/id #uuid "03136157-253b-5e52-a825-c65e603018ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "64" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131707}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "03136157-253b-5e52-a825-c65e603018ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "64" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131707} :answer-field/id #uuid "da61fd60-b90c-54fb-a28f-baa9844cebb4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131702}] :question/id #uuid "df0fc1c3-daea-50ab-b3dc-d1aac4fefd9e" :question/math-academy-id "q-40395" :question/problem "Evaluate the mixed integral\n\n$∬_(D)[∫_{0}^{y}12zdx]dA$\nwhere the region $D$ in the $yz$-plane is given by\n$D = {(y, z): 0 \\le y \\le 2, 0 \\le z \\le 4 - y^{2}}$."}]}
